{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The Pacific end of Union Pacific's route to the Northwest: the Oregon Railway and Navigation Company's main line from Portland up the south bank of the Columbia and over the Blue Mountains to Huntington, Oregon, where it met the Oregon Short Line (see union_pacific_oregon_short_line.geojson) to complete an Omaha-Portland through route on Dec 1 1884. Three features over the same corridor, split at the 1910 ownership change per CLAUDE.md Rule 4: the two OR&N-era features (Portland-Pendleton from 1882, Pendleton-Huntington from 1884, both in the OR&N's colour) and one Union Pacific feature over the whole route for 1910-1997, coloured UP because that is who ran the trains. The OR&N era is drawn as two features because the two halves opened two years apart -- the river line was complete by the end of 1882, the Blue Mountain crossing not until late 1884.", "source": "utahrails.net (ORy&N / ORR&N / O-WR&N timeline -- section opening dates and mileages, 1896 reorganization, Nov 1910 O-WRR&N consolidation, Jan 1 1936 UP lease), Oregon Encyclopedia (Oregon Railway & Navigation Company -- Nov 25 1884 golden spike at Huntington, Dec 1 1884 through service to Omaha), Wikipedia (Oregon Railroad and Navigation Company, Portland Rose (train), City of Portland (train)), pnwc-nrhs.org (Oregon-Washington Railroad & Navigation Company). Geometry from OpenStreetMap route relations 7525013, 7524906, 8726026.", "pulled": "20260826", "not_yet_sourced": ["Whether scheduled passenger trains ran on each staged section before the through line opened -- Portland-Pendleton is drawn from 1882 for its whole length on the strength of the last link opening Nov 20 1882, not on a timetable.", "Headways throughout: every headway_min here is an estimate, not read off a timetable.", "How much of the Columbia River line was relocated for The Dalles Dam (1957) and John Day Dam (1968), and where -- asserted as the reason the traced river half runs ~10 mi short of the documented mileage, but not measured against a pre-dam alignment.", "Local and branch passenger service off this main line (Joseph branch, Wallowa, Heppner, Homedale) is not drawn."]}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Oregon Railway and Navigation Company", "line": "Oregon Railway and Navigation: Portland \u2013 Pendleton", "id": "orn-portland-pendleton", "color": "#4A6FA5", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 40, "speed_source": "estimated: 1880s-1900s steam schedules over a water-level river route, roughly 220 mi in 9-10 hours including stops; unsourced", "geometry_quality": "Portland - Pendleton traced at 354.3 km / 220.1 mi against a documented 230.6 mi (Portland-Bonneville 40.67 + Bonneville-The Dalles 46.21 + The Dalles-Celilo 13.80 + Celilo-Blalock 32.70 + Blalock-Umatilla 53.40 + Umatilla-Pendleton 43.80, utahrails). The traced line is ~10 mi short, which is what the post-dam relocations along the Columbia would be expected to produce -- the modern line cuts several of the 1880s riverbank meanders. Traced on real track from OpenStreetMap: UP route relations 7525013 (Portland Subdivision), 7524906 (La Grande Subdivision) and 8726026 (Huntington Subdivision), pulled from the OSM API and assembled by weighted shortest path over the way graph (supplementary railway=rail ways used only to bridge the relations' own member gaps at Pendleton and in Portland). Every vertex is an OSM node on existing track -- there are no straight-line approximation legs; the longest gap between consecutive nodes is 3.0 km, on tangent track near Boardman. The east end stops 0.28 km short of the Huntington depot site, the west end 0.06 km from Portland Union Station. ERA CAVEAT (CLAUDE.md Rule 2): this is the modern alignment, not the 1880s one. The Columbia River portion was relocated where The Dalles Dam (1957) and John Day Dam (1968) reservoirs drowned the original riverbank grade, so for the pre-1957 years the drawn line sits above and inland of the track actually used.", "segments": [{"start": 1882, "end": 1884, "headway_min": 720, "note": "The OR&N's Columbia River line: The Dalles-Celilo (the old Oregon Steam Navigation portage, 1863) extended east to Blalock Nov 23 1880 and Umatilla Apr 16 1881, Umatilla-Pendleton opened Sept 12 1882, Bonneville-The Dalles May 21 1882 and the last link Portland-Bonneville Nov 20 1882 -- so the whole Portland-Pendleton line was open by the end of 1882. Trains in these first years worked local and steamboat-connecting traffic; there was no through eastern connection yet. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["Segment-level: opening/corporate dates checked 2026-08-26 against the utahrails ORy&N / ORR&N / O-WR&N timeline (which gives each section's opening date and mileage); headway and speed are unsourced estimates.", "https://utahrails.net/up/orn-owrn-timeline.html", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oregon_Railroad_and_Navigation_Company"]}, {"start": 1884, "end": 1896, "headway_min": 720, "note": "Through service began Dec 1 1884: with the Blue Mountain line finished to Huntington (golden spike Nov 25 1884) the OR&N became the Pacific end of the Union Pacific's fourth transcontinental route, Omaha-Portland via the Oregon Short Line. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["Segment-level: opening/corporate dates checked 2026-08-26 against the utahrails ORy&N / ORR&N / O-WR&N timeline (which gives each section's opening date and mileage); headway and speed are unsourced estimates.", "https://utahrails.net/up/orn-owrn-timeline.html", "https://www.oregonencyclopedia.org/articles/oregon_railroad_and_navigation_company/"]}, {"start": 1896, "end": 1910, "headway_min": 720, "note": "Reorganized Aug 17 1896 as the Oregon Railroad and Navigation Company after the OR&N's receivership; Union Pacific bought a majority stake in 1898, and the line was folded into the new Oregon-Washington Railroad and Navigation Company in Nov-Dec 1910 (see the companion Union Pacific feature over the same geometry). Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["Segment-level: opening/corporate dates checked 2026-08-26 against the utahrails ORy&N / ORR&N / O-WR&N timeline (which gives each section's opening date and mileage); headway and speed are unsourced estimates.", "https://utahrails.net/up/orn-owrn-timeline.html", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oregon_Railroad_and_Navigation_Company"]}]}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-122.675936, 45.528692], [-122.674512, 45.527749], [-122.671009, 45.526902], [-122.668044, 45.528117], [-122.66763, 45.528863], [-122.671327, 45.531149], [-122.67477, 45.536037], [-122.675345, 45.538351], [-122.67952, 45.540946], [-122.683123, 45.544308], [-122.700383, 45.563099], [-122.710085, 45.56941], [-122.711934, 45.571378], [-122.712316, 45.593041], [-122.658502, 45.578218], [-122.616945, 45.570096], [-122.592594, 45.567011], [-122.492696, 45.549707], [-122.475911, 45.548062], [-122.457743, 45.547992], [-122.445535, 45.545776], [-122.428592, 45.548469], [-122.424666, 45.548546], [-122.420588, 45.547833], [-122.409865, 45.544065], [-122.397232, 45.541837], [-122.386808, 45.541299], [-122.378761, 45.542308], [-122.362967, 45.542745], [-122.343547, 45.541353], [-122.330273, 45.538041], [-122.306657, 45.539009], [-122.295006, 45.541227], [-122.289515, 45.541557], [-122.284241, 45.541024], [-122.275104, 45.541759], [-122.258458, 45.53982], [-122.251038, 45.54112], [-122.225472, 45.541881], [-122.21875, 45.541335], [-122.206953, 45.542947], [-122.200687, 45.54528], [-122.196306, 45.548634], [-122.183825, 45.554956], [-122.167057, 45.565646], [-122.156538, 45.571537], [-122.151269, 45.572585], [-122.143305, 45.575443], [-122.13539, 45.575403], [-122.120982, 45.577204], [-122.102068, 45.581524], [-122.097652, 45.583209], [-122.092219, 45.584386], [-122.086181, 45.58709], [-122.076881, 45.589571], [-122.061818, 45.591719], [-122.058483, 45.593314], [-122.04183, 45.604411], [-122.030226, 45.609807], [-