-Huntington running times of roughly 9-10 hours over 404 mi, including the Blue Mountain grade; unsourced", "geometry_quality": "Whole route Portland - Huntington traced at 632.3 km / 392.9 mi against a documented 404.1 mi (sum of the utahrails section mileages) -- 97%, the shortfall almost all on the Columbia River half. Traced on real track from OpenStreetMap: UP route relations 7525013 (Portland Subdivision), 7524906 (La Grande Subdivision) and 8726026 (Huntington Subdivision), pulled from the OSM API and assembled by weighted shortest path over the way graph (supplementary railway=rail ways used only to bridge the relations' own member gaps at Pendleton and in Portland). Every vertex is an OSM node on existing track -- there are no straight-line approximation legs; the longest gap between consecutive nodes is 3.0 km, on tangent track near Boardman. The east end stops 0.28 km short of the Huntington depot site, the west end 0.06 km from Portland Union Station. ERA CAVEAT (CLAUDE.md Rule 2): this is the modern alignment, not the 1880s one. The Columbia River portion was relocated where The Dalles Dam (1957) and John Day Dam (1968) reservoirs drowned the original riverbank grade, so for the pre-1957 years the drawn line sits above and inland of the track actually used. Straight-line repair 2026-08-29: 2 ruled segments totalling 25 mi replaced with OpenStreetMap-traced rail geometry (see geometry_repairs for the OSM lines each one followed).", "segments": [{"start": 1910, "end": 1930, "headway_min": 480, "note": "Consolidated into the Oregon-Washington Railroad and Navigation Company (a Union Pacific subsidiary) in Nov-Dec 1910, with 14 other Harriman properties in Oregon and Washington; the Portland Limited and secondary trains worked the Omaha/Chicago-Portland gateway. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["Segment-level: opening/corporate dates checked 2026-08-26 against the utahrails ORy&N / ORR&N / O-WR&N timeline (which gives each section's opening date and mileage); headway and speed are unsourced estimates.", "https://utahrails.net/up/orn-owrn-timeline.html", "https://www.pnwc-nrhs.org/hs_or_n.html"]}, {"start": 1930, "end": 1935, "headway_min": 480, "note": "The Portland Rose replaced the Portland Limited as UP's flagship to the Northwest on Sept 12 1930. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["Segment-level: named-train dates checked 2026-08-26 against web sources for the Portland Rose (launched Sept 12 1930 replacing the Portland Limited; last run Apr 30 1971) and the City of Portland (first trip June 6 1935); headway is an unsourced estimate.", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portland_Rose_(train)"]}, {"start": 1935, "end": 1971, "headway_min": 360, "note": "Streamliner era: the City of Portland made its first trip June 6 1935 (M-10001) and ran alongside the Portland Rose; UP leased the Oregon-Washington properties outright effective Jan 1 1936. All Union Pacific passenger service over this line ended with the Portland Rose's last run on Apr 30 1971, the day before Amtrak. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["Segment-level: named-train dates checked 2026-08-26 against web sources for the Portland Rose (launched Sept 12 1930 replacing the Portland Limited; last run Apr 30 1971) and the City of Portland (first trip June 6 1935); headway is an unsourced estimate.", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_of_Portland_(train)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portland_Rose_(train)", "https://utahrails.net/up/orn-owrn-timeline.html"]}, {"start": 1977, "end": 1997, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "Amtrak's Pioneer revived service over this corridor in 1977 after a six-year gap with no passenger trains at all, running Portland-Pendleton-Huntington-Boise until it was discontinued May 10 1997; the train itself is drawn separately in data/amtrak_pioneer.geojson. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the Pioneer's 1977 start and May 10 1997 discontinuation are carried over